The provided code snippet is a C# class named `WaitingGroupState` that appears to be part of a larger system for managing group states in a media playback context. The class has several methods, including `SessionLeaving`, which is called when a session leaves a group.

To write unit tests for this class, we can use a testing framework like xUnit. We'll need to create test cases that cover the different scenarios that the `SessionLeaving` method can encounter.

Here's an example of how we might write a test for the `SessionLeaving` method:
```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using MediaBrowser.Controller.SyncPlay.GroupStates;
using MediaBrowser.Controller.Session;

namespace MediaBrowser.Controller.SyncPlay.Tests
{
    public class WaitingGroupStateTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public void SessionLeaving_LogsDebugMessage_WhenResumePlayingIsTrue()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<WaitingGroupState>>();
            var contextMock = new Mock<IGroupStateContext>();
            var sessionInfo = new SessionInfo(null, null);
            sessionInfo.Id = Guid.NewGuid();
            var waitingGroupState = new WaitingGroupState(new LoggerFactory());

            waitingGroupState.ResumePlaying = true;
            contextMock.Setup(c => c.GroupId).Returns(Guid.NewGuid());

            // Act
            waitingGroupState.SessionLeaving(contextMock.Object, GroupStateType.Playing, sessionInfo, default);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogDebug(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }

        [Fact]
        public void SessionLeaving_LogsDebugMessage_WhenResumePlayingIsFalse()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger<WaitingGroupState>>();
            var contextMock = new Mock<IGroupStateContext>();
            var sessionInfo = new SessionInfo(null, null);
            sessionInfo.Id = Guid.NewGuid();
            var waitingGroupState = new WaitingGroupState(new LoggerFactory());

            waitingGroupState.ResumePlaying = false;
            contextMock.Setup(c => c.GroupId).Returns(Guid.NewGuid());

            // Act
            waitingGroupState.SessionLeaving(contextMock.Object, GroupStateType.Playing, sessionInfo, default);

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogDebug(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we're using Moq to create mock implementations of the `ILogger<WaitingGroupState>` and `IGroupStateContext` interfaces. We're then creating a `WaitingGroupState` instance and setting up the mock context to return a specific group ID.

In the `Act` section, we're calling the `SessionLeaving` method on the `WaitingGroupState` instance, passing in the mock context, a `GroupStateType` value, and a `SessionInfo` object.

Finally, in the `Assert` section, we're verifying that the `LogDebug` method was called on the mock logger, using the `Verify` method provided by Moq.

Note that this is just one possible way to write unit tests for the `WaitingGroupState` class. Depending on the specific requirements of your system, you may need to write additional tests to cover other scenarios or edge cases.
